<Window x:Class="ConnectIVRTester.ShareScriptsHelpDialog"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Sharing Scripts &amp; Batches"
        Width="560" Height="520"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner">
    <Grid Margin="16">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="0" VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
            <StackPanel>
                <TextBlock Text="Sharing Scripts &amp; Batches" FontSize="16" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,10"/>

                <TextBlock Text="Scripts and batches are just .json files - sharing one is as simple as sending the file." TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

                <TextBlock Text="To share a script or batch with someone" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,4">
                    1. Select it in Script Selector (or Run Batch) and note where it's saved -
                    the location is shown under the list, or click the item to see its path.
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Text="2. Copy that .json file and send it however you'd normally share a file (email, chat, USB drive, network folder)." TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                <TextBlock Text="3. The recipient saves it anywhere on their own machine." TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12"/>

                <TextBlock Text="To bring a received script into your own app" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
                    Use the <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="Import Script..."/> button next to
                    "+ New Script" in Script Selector. Browse to the .json file you were sent -
                    it'll register under your currently selected Environment and show up in
                    your Script Selector immediately. No manual file editing required.
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Text="Batches" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,12">
                    A batch file references its scripts by file path, so if you share a batch,
                    share its referenced scripts too (or make sure the recipient already has
                    scripts with matching names/paths). Batches don't currently have their own
                    Import button - for now, the simplest approach for a shared batch is for
                    each teammate to recreate it locally via Create Batch once they've imported
                    the underlying scripts.
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Text="For a whole team sharing the same scripts" FontWeight="Bold" Margin="0,0,0,4"/>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,4">
                    If several people want to stay in sync automatically rather than re-sending
                    files by hand, point Save Script's "Browse..." location at a shared network
                    folder everyone can reach (e.g. \\server\share\DialScript Scripts\) instead
                    of a local folder. Anyone who saves there is editing the same underlying
                    files - and everyone else can use Import Script to pull the latest version
                    in whenever they need it.
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,4">
                    A few things worth knowing with this approach:
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="12,0,0,4">
                    • Each person's own Script Selector list is still local - saving to a shared
                    folder doesn't automatically add it to everyone else's list, they still need
                    to Import it once.
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="12,0,0,4">
                    • If two people edit and save the same script at the same time, the last
                    save wins - there's no merge/conflict warning.
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="12,0,0,12">
                    • Everyone needs the shared drive/folder mapped and reachable for their
                    scripts to load - if it's offline, those scripts just won't be available
                    until it's back.
                </TextBlock>
            </StackPanel>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <Button Grid.Row="1" Content="Close" Padding="14,4" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                Margin="0,12,0,0" Click="Close_Click"/>
    </Grid>
</Window>
